<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hsipaw State</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1959 CE (approx.) · capital Hsipaw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern Shan state on the Dokhtawady river whose last ruler, Sao Kya Seng, disappeared after the Burmese military coup of 1962.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hsipaw, Shan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thibaw-min">Thibaw</a>, was founded as a Shan principality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myitnge-river">Dokhtawady</a> valley of the northern Shan plateau; tradition dates its ruling house to the 14th century. For long periods it was associated with the larger state of Onbaung, whose name it sometimes used.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The saopha of Hsipaw ruled from a haw, or palace, overseeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> growing and trade along the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Hsipaw was a tributary of the Burmese crown and supplied consorts to the royal court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-hkun-hseng">Sao Hkun Hseng</a>, ruler from 1886, cooperated with the British and modernised his state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hsipaw became one of the British protected Shan states and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a> in 1922. Its ruler surrendered powers in 1959. The last saopha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-kya-seng">Sao Kya Seng</a>, and his Austrian wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inge-sargent">Inge Sargent</a>, whose memoir told their story, were victims of the 1962 coup; he disappeared in army custody.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hsipaw-state.jpg" alt="Hsipaw State" width="320" height="368"><figcaption>Image: User:Jolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shan_States-Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1959 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hsipaw</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sao Hkun Hseng, Sao Kya Seng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hsipaw, Thibaw, Onbaung</dd></dl></aside></div>

